    id: 'w2-sos',
    kind: 'concept',
    title: 'Systems of systems: Maier’s two independences and five characteristics; the four SoS types',
    paragraphs: [
      'Every element of a system can itself be regarded as a system, so SEBoK points out that “system of systems” could be applied to anything and would then be a superfluous term. Maier (1998) gave it a sharper meaning: an SoS is an assemblage of components that may individually be regarded as systems and that have two additional properties. Operational independence: if the SoS were taken apart, the component systems must be able to operate usefully on their own, fulfilling customer-operator purposes. Managerial independence: the components not only can but do operate independently — they are separately acquired and integrated and keep a continuing operational existence independent of the SoS. Note the plural: the test applies to every component system, so an argument that shows only some components to be independent is incomplete.',
      'SEBoK’s SoS knowledge area lists Maier’s five key characteristics — operational independence, managerial independence, geographical distribution, emergent behaviour and evolutionary development processes — and stresses that they are characteristics, not criteria. R08 uses the word “criteria” for the first two: it reserves the term SoS for systems that “meet the criteria of operational and managerial independence” (R08 p.833). The two statements are consistent — the two independences are the classifying criteria; the five-item list is a set of characteristics. Only the first two distinguish an SoS: a system without both is not an SoS, however complex or geographically spread its components are. Maier himself is quoted as saying that complexity and geographic distribution are “not the appropriate taxonomic classifiers”. Emergence is noted as common in SoS, particularly those built from several large existing systems, given the challenge in time and resources of exercising every logical thread across their functions, capabilities and data; unintended interactions become serious when, for example, safety is threatened.',
      'The ISO/IEC/IEEE 21839 definition quoted in SEBoK puts the emphasis on purpose: an SoS is a set of systems or system elements that interact to provide a unique capability that none of the constituent systems can accomplish on its own; each constituent is a useful system by itself, with its own development, management goals and resources. SEBoK adds that forming an SoS is not necessarily permanent — it can be a matter of necessity for specific goals.',
      'When an SoS is recognised and treated as a system in its own right, it can be described as one of four types. The taxonomy is not Maier’s alone: R08 credits the four kinds of SoS to Dahmann, Lane and Rebovich (2008), and R09 cites Maier (1998), Dahmann and Baldwin (2008) and ISO/IEC/IEEE 21839 for it, noting that it is now presented in ISO 21841. The types differ in the degree of independence of the constituents and in where the SoS objectives come from. In a directed SoS the constituents keep the ability to operate independently but their normal operational mode is subordinated to the centrally managed purpose. An acknowledged SoS has recognised objectives, a designated manager and resources, while the constituents keep their own ownership, objectives, funding and development, and changes rest on cooperative agreements. In a collaborative SoS the constituents interact more or less voluntarily to fulfil agreed central purposes. A virtual SoS has neither a central management authority nor a centrally agreed purpose; large-scale behaviour emerges and is maintained by relatively invisible mechanisms. Real SoS usually combine types and change type over time, and many exist unrecognised until something makes them significant — SEBoK calls these “discovered” SoS.',
    ],
    citations: [{ reading: 'R08', pages: '832–833' }, { reading: 'R09', pages: '866–869' }],
